<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Unattended install for a BARE-METAL x64 capture worker, served from the PXE box.

  The sibling at ../../local-worker/autounattend.xml is the proven recipe, and this is that recipe
  adapted for real hardware on a real LAN. Four differences, each of which matters:

  1. NO PLAINTEXT PASSWORD. The arm64 file embeds `witness`/`witness` and justifies it as "a disposable
     local VM behind QEMU user-mode networking, reachable solely through forwarded ports". None of that
     is true of a mini PC on your LAN, so the justification does not travel with the file. The account
     is created with a BLANK password instead, which is the same design the rest of the fleet already
     depends on: `LimitBlankPasswordUse=1` (a Windows default) confines a blank-password account to
     CONSOLE logon, so it cannot be reached over SMB, RDP or password SSH at all. That makes the account
     strictly narrower than a password would, not wider — and nothing secret is ever committed here.
     Provisioning re-asserts and VERIFIES that invariant afterwards; see provision-nvda-worker.ps1.

  2. NO VIRTIO DRIVERS. Those paths point at UTM's support ISO (`E:\Drivers\...`) and exist because a
     QEMU guest has no inbox driver for its own disk or NIC. Real hardware does. Leaving them in would
     fail the windowsPE pass looking for a drive that is not there.

  3. ComputerName is `*`, not `A11Y-WORKER`. Twelve machines answering to one NetBIOS name is a name
     conflict on a real network; the arm64 file could hardcode it because there is one VM behind NAT.
     Workers are identified by address in ansible/inventory.yml, so a generated name costs nothing.

  4. The TPM/SecureBoot/CPU bypasses are KEPT, and here they are load-bearing rather than a VM
     workaround: the 6th and 7th generation boxes in this fleet (EliteDesk 800 G2/G3) are not on
     Windows 11's supported-CPU list at all, and would refuse to install. They are harmless on the
     8th-gen machines that would pass anyway.

  ============================ THIS WIPES DISK 0, WITHOUT ASKING ============================
  `WillWipeDisk` on DiskID 0 is what makes the install hands-off, and it is why this file must only
  ever be served to a machine you intend to become a worker. Point your PXE server's boot entry at
  the machines you have set aside, not at a default-for-everything entry.
  ==========================================================================================

  UAC stays at its DEFAULT, deliberately, exactly as the arm64 file explains: with UAC off every
  process runs at high integrity, and the capture pipeline depends on NVDA (nvda_noUIAccess.exe) and
  the browser sitting at the SAME integrity level. Provisioning gets elevation from a RunLevel Highest
  scheduled task, which elevates with no prompt — and a prompt would be fatal, since UAC dialogs render
  on the secure desktop where no automation can reach them.
-->
<unattend xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:unattend" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State">

  <settings pass="windowsPE">
    <!-- The locale MUST be one the INSTALL MEDIA actually carries, and this is the defect that made
         every unattended install fall back to a fully interactive one.

         Our media is Win11_23H2_EnglishInternational_x64v2, whose volume label is
         CCCOMA_X64FRE_EN-GB_DV9: it ships en-GB resources and no en-US ones. Asking for en-US
         therefore fails at the point Setup tries to load them, and Setup's response is not an error
         but a FALLBACK to the language-selection page. From the console that is indistinguishable
         from the answer file never being read, which is how it was misdiagnosed repeatedly: the
         edition name, the boot mode, iVentoy's injection and the payload server were each blamed and
         each was fine.

         The proof is in Setup's own log, which reports success right up to this point:

           Found usable unattend file for pass [windowsPE] at [C:\autounattend.xml]
           Successfully deserialized and validated unattend file
           Product key is valid ... User accepted the EULA in the unattend file
           SetSetupKeyboardLayout: Failed to set the key board layout to [en-US]      <- here
           CLanguages::v_InitLanguage - Failed to load en-US resources with error 2

         Error 2 is FILE NOT FOUND. Check the media before changing these: the volume label encodes
         the language, and `Callback_Locale_*` in X:\Windows\Panther\setupact.log names the failure. -->
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core-WinPE" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS">
      <SetupUILanguage>
        <UILanguage>en-GB</UILanguage>
      </SetupUILanguage>
      <InputLocale>en-GB</InputLocale>
      <SystemLocale>en-GB</SystemLocale>
      <UILanguage>en-GB</UILanguage>
      <UserLocale>en-GB</UserLocale>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Setup" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS">
      <Diagnostics>
        <OptIn>false</OptIn>
      </Diagnostics>

      <!-- UEFI/GPT layout: ESP, MSR, then Windows across the remainder. -->
      <DiskConfiguration>
        <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        <Disk wcm:action="add">
          <DiskID>0</DiskID>
          <WillWipeDisk>true</WillWipeDisk>
          <CreatePartitions>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>1</Order>
              <Type>EFI</Type>
              <Size>300</Size>
            </CreatePartition>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>2</Order>
              <Type>MSR</Type>
              <Size>16</Size>
            </CreatePartition>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>3</Order>
              <Type>Primary</Type>
              <Extend>true</Extend>
            </CreatePartition>
          </CreatePartitions>
          <ModifyPartitions>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>1</Order>
              <PartitionID>1</PartitionID>
              <Format>FAT32</Format>
              <Label>System</Label>
            </ModifyPartition>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>2</Order>
              <PartitionID>2</PartitionID>
            </ModifyPartition>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>3</Order>
              <PartitionID>3</PartitionID>
              <Format>NTFS</Format>
              <Label>Windows</Label>
              <Letter>C</Letter>
            </ModifyPartition>
          </ModifyPartitions>
        </Disk>
      </DiskConfiguration>

      <!-- /IMAGE/NAME must match an image NAME in the media's own install.wim EXACTLY, and the
           value is a property of the ISO rather than of Windows. Read it, never assume it:

             mount -o loop,ro <iso> /mnt
             tail -c 200000 /mnt/sources/install.wim | tr -d '\0' | grep -oE '<NAME>[^<]*</NAME>'

           This said "Windows 11 Professional". On Win11_23H2_EnglishInternational_x64v2 both NAME
           and DESCRIPTION are "Windows 11 Pro", and "Professional" is Windows 7-era naming that
           appears nowhere in that WIM, so it named no image at all.

           HONESTY ABOUT WHAT THIS DID AND DID NOT CAUSE: it was found while chasing an interactive
           install, and written up at the time as the cause of it. It was not — the locale mismatch
           above was, proven afterwards from Setup's own log. This value was genuinely wrong and had
           to be fixed, but it was never demonstrated to produce a symptom, because the locale bug
           stopped Setup before the image was ever selected. Treat it as a latent defect corrected,
           not as a diagnosis.

           NOTE FOR THE NEXT EDITOR: an XML comment may not contain two consecutive hyphens. Using
           them as a dash here broke the file a third time in this repo's history, and a malformed
           answer file fails in exactly the same silent way as a wrong edition name. Validate with
           `python3 -c "import xml.dom.minidom as m; m.parse('autounattend.xml')"` before pushing.

           The generic key below is Microsoft's public KMS key for Pro, so this file always meant
           Pro; only the spelling was wrong.

           Keep this block byte-for-byte simple: substituting /IMAGE/INDEX here, or adding a
           comment inside <InstallFrom>, made Setup reject the whole answer file the same way. -->
      <ImageInstall>
        <OSImage>
          <InstallFrom>
            <MetaData wcm:action="add">
              <Key>/IMAGE/NAME</Key>
              <Value>Windows 11 Pro</Value>
            </MetaData>
          </InstallFrom>
          <InstallTo>
            <DiskID>0</DiskID>
            <PartitionID>3</PartitionID>
          </InstallTo>
          <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        </OSImage>
      </ImageInstall>

      <!-- Not a VM workaround here: the 6th/7th-gen boxes in this fleet are not on Windows 11's
           supported-CPU list and Setup refuses them outright without these. Harmless on 8th-gen. -->
      <RunSynchronous>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\System\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassCPUCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 0x00000001 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>2</Order>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\System\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassRAMCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 0x00000001 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>3</Order>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\System\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassSecureBootCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 0x00000001 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>4</Order>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\System\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassTPMCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 0x00000001 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>5</Order>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\System\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassDiskCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 0x00000001 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <!-- BypassStorageCheck is the name Setup actually reads; BypassDiskCheck was inherited from the
             arm64 file and appears in no Microsoft documentation. Both are kept: an ignored registry
             value costs nothing, and a missing one costs the install. -->
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>6</Order>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\System\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassStorageCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 0x00000001 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <!-- The install-time bypasses above do not cover the UPGRADE path, which is what a later feature
             update takes. Without this a box installs happily and then refuses 25H2 with "this PC doesn't
             meet the minimum requirements", months later, on a machine nobody is watching. -->
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>7</Order>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\System\Setup\MoSetup /v AllowUpgradesWithUnsupportedTPMOrCPU /t REG_DWORD /d 0x00000001 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
      </RunSynchronous>

      <UserData>
        <AcceptEula>true</AcceptEula>
        <FullName>a11y-witness</FullName>
        <Organization>a11y-witness</Organization>
        <!-- NO product key, deliberately, and the element is present-but-empty rather than absent.
             `/IMAGE/NAME` above already selects the edition, which is the only job a key was doing
             here: we do not want activation, and an unactivated Windows is fully functional for
             capture.

             It used to carry the generic Windows 11 Pro KMS client key
             (W269N-WFGWX-YVC9B-4J6C9-T83GX). That worked while the media shipped a single
             install.wim, and started failing with "the unattended answer file contains an invalid
             product key" once install.wim was SPLIT into install.swm parts to fit the ISO's file-size
             limit. Setup validates the key against the edition it resolved from the split image, and
             the two stopped agreeing. Naming the edition and omitting the key removes the
             disagreement rather than trying to satisfy both halves of it.

             The `Key` element must be PRESENT and EMPTY, exactly like the blank password below.
             Omitting it entirely fails differently and just as fatally: Setup stops with "Windows
             cannot read the <ProductKey> setting from the unattend answer file". Empty means "no key,
             do not ask"; absent means "malformed". `WillShowUI=Never` is what suppresses the "enter
             your product key" page, which on a headless fleet is a hang nobody is there to clear. -->
        <ProductKey>
          <Key></Key>
          <WillShowUI>Never</WillShowUI>
        </ProductKey>
      </UserData>
    </component>
  </settings>

  <settings pass="specialize">
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS">
      <!-- `*` generates a unique name. Twelve machines called A11Y-WORKER is a NetBIOS conflict on a
           real network; the workers are addressed by IP from ansible/inventory.yml regardless. -->
      <ComputerName>*</ComputerName>
      <TimeZone>GMT Standard Time</TimeZone>
    </component>
    <!-- The windowsPE LabConfig keys live in the INSTALLER's registry and do not survive into the
         installed system. Written again here so the running OS carries them, which is what a feature
         update and any in-place repair will consult. Without this the box installs on unsupported
         hardware and then quietly stops being able to update itself.

         BypassNRO removes the "you must connect to the internet and sign in with a Microsoft account"
         wall that 24H2 reintroduced at OOBE. The LocalAccount + HideOnlineAccountScreens below usually
         carry it, but usually is not a property you want on a fleet you cannot see the screen of. -->
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Deployment" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS">
      <RunSynchronous>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <Description>Persist the hardware bypasses into the installed OS</Description>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\SYSTEM\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassTPMCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 0x00000001 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>2</Order>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\SYSTEM\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassSecureBootCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 0x00000001 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>3</Order>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\SYSTEM\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassCPUCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 0x00000001 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>4</Order>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\SYSTEM\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassRAMCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 0x00000001 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>5</Order>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\SYSTEM\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassStorageCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 0x00000001 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>6</Order>
          <Description>Allow feature updates on unsupported TPM/CPU</Description>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\SYSTEM\Setup\MoSetup /v AllowUpgradesWithUnsupportedTPMOrCPU /t REG_DWORD /d 0x00000001 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>7</Order>
          <Description>No Microsoft account / network wall at OOBE</Description>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\OOBE /v BypassNRO /t REG_DWORD /d 0x00000001 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
      </RunSynchronous>
    </component>
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Security-SPP-UX" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS">
      <SkipAutoActivation>true</SkipAutoActivation>
    </component>
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-SQMApi" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS">
      <CEIPEnabled>0</CEIPEnabled>
    </component>
  </settings>

  <settings pass="oobeSystem">
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS">
      <InputLocale>en-GB</InputLocale>
      <SystemLocale>en-GB</SystemLocale>
      <UILanguage>en-GB</UILanguage>
      <UserLocale>en-GB</UserLocale>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS">
      <UserAccounts>
        <LocalAccounts>
          <LocalAccount wcm:action="add">
            <Name>witness</Name>
            <DisplayName>witness</DisplayName>
            <Group>Administrators</Group>
            <!-- BLANK, and empty rather than absent: Setup wants the element. See the header — a
                 blank-password account is confined to console logon by LimitBlankPasswordUse, which
                 is what makes credential-free auto-logon safe on a networked box. -->
            <Password>
              <Value></Value>
              <PlainText>true</PlainText>
            </Password>
          </LocalAccount>
        </LocalAccounts>
      </UserAccounts>

      <!-- The whole reason this file exists. NVDA is a GUI application: it needs a real logged-on
           desktop session. No auto-logon means no session after a reboot, which means captures
           silently return nothing at all — and on a headless fleet nobody is there to log in. -->
      <AutoLogon>
        <Enabled>true</Enabled>
        <Username>witness</Username>
        <Password>
          <Value></Value>
          <PlainText>true</PlainText>
        </Password>
        <LogonCount>2147483647</LogonCount>
      </AutoLogon>

      <OOBE>
        <HideEULAPage>true</HideEULAPage>
        <HideOEMRegistrationScreen>true</HideOEMRegistrationScreen>
        <HideOnlineAccountScreens>true</HideOnlineAccountScreens>
        <HideLocalAccountScreen>true</HideLocalAccountScreen>
        <HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>true</HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>
        <ProtectYourPC>3</ProtectYourPC>
      </OOBE>

      <FirstLogonCommands>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <Description>Disable hibernation</Description>
          <CommandLine>cmd /c POWERCFG -H OFF</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <!-- Never leave the console locked: a locked desktop makes NVDA silent. Belt and braces
             alongside provisioning, which also turns the screensaver off. -->
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>2</Order>
          <Description>Disable screensaver (it steals foreground mid-capture)</Description>
          <CommandLine>reg add "HKCU\Control Panel\Desktop" /v ScreenSaveActive /t REG_SZ /d 0 /f</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <!-- Fetch the payload over HTTP rather than looking for it on the install media.
             ==================== SET THE ADDRESS BELOW BEFORE USE ====================
             `$h` below is 203.0.113.10 -- an RFC 5737 DOCUMENTATION address, not this fleet's real
             control-plane host (#86: a real address sat here in tracked source; RFC 5737 is reserved so
             it can never resolve to anything, the same replacement #83 used elsewhere in this repo).
             It WILL fail to fetch, on purpose: a box that silently boots against a stale or wrong host
             is a worse failure than one that visibly hangs retrying an address that cannot answer.
             Replace it with your control plane's real address before generating install media.

             iVentoy injects files to X:, which is the WinPE RAM disk and is GONE by the time this runs,
             so scanning drive letters (which the UTM path does, from a support ISO that stays attached)
             finds nothing here. Fetching also means one small file to serve rather than a repacked ISO,
             and this project already has scar tissue from El Torito boot catalogues.

             The box PXE-booted from this host moments ago, so it can certainly reach it.
             `serve-bootstrap.sh` on the control plane serves exactly these two files. -->
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>3</Order>
          <Description>Fetch the bootstrap payload from the control plane</Description>
          <CommandLine>powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "$h='http://203.0.113.10:8099'; $d='C:\a11y'; New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force $d | Out-Null; foreach($f in 'first-boot.cmd','bootstrap-windows-worker.ps1','operator-key.pub'){ for($i=0;$i -lt 60;$i++){ try{ Invoke-WebRequest -UseBasicParsing -Uri ($h+'/'+$f) -OutFile ($d+'\'+$f); break }catch{ Start-Sleep -Seconds 5 } } }"</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
        <!-- Then hand off. first-boot.cmd waits for DHCP, stages the operator key, and runs the bootstrap
             elevated via a RunLevel Highest scheduled task, because a UAC prompt here would render on the secure
             desktop where no automation can reach it. After this the box is reachable by Ansible and needs
             no console visit at all. -->
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>4</Order>
          <Description>Run the worker bootstrap elevated, without a UAC prompt</Description>
          <CommandLine>cmd /c if exist C:\a11y\first-boot.cmd start /wait C:\a11y\first-boot.cmd</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
      </FirstLogonCommands>
    </component>
  </settings>
</unattend>
